 {d:2,t:"mem",best:1,st:"Cables under the sea",q:"What share of the data that crosses the oceans travels through undersea cables?",o:["Over 99%","About half","About 10%","None; it goes by satellite"],a:0,why:"Hundreds of fibre-optic cables, most about as thick as a garden hose, carry pulses of laser light between continents."},
 {d:2,t:"mem",st:"School around the world",q:"At what age do children in Finland start school?",o:["Seven","Four","Five","Ten"],a:0,cc:"fi",why:"In England many start at four. Finland still scores above the average of rich countries, with short days and little homework."},
 {d:3,t:"map",st:"Nalanda",q:"Click Nalanda, in today’s Bihar.",ll:[85.44,25.14],km:400,cc:"in",why:"Nalanda lies on the Ganges plain in north-east India. Its brick ruins became a World Heritage Site in 2016."},
 {d:3,t:"mem",st:"Nalanda",q:"Which Chinese monk walked to Nalanda in the 630s and carried hundreds of texts home?",o:["Xuanzang","Confucius","Zheng He","Cai Lun"],a:0,cc:"cn",why:"He walked from Chang’an, studied for years and visited the court of King Harsha too."},
 {d:3,t:"mem",best:1,st:"The road of paper",q:"About how long did paper take to travel from China to Europe?",o:["About 1,000 years","About 10 years","About 100 years","About 3,000 years"],a:0,why:"Cai Lun reported paper-making in AD 105; it reached Samarkand in the 700s, Baghdad by the 790s and Spain by the 1100s."},
 {d:3,t:"why",st:"The road of paper",q:"Why did paper mills in Baghdad help so many Greek books survive?",o:["Books became far cheaper than on parchment, so scholars could copy and translate more","Paper never burns","Greek books were banned elsewhere","Paper was used only for maths"],a:0,cc:"iq",why:"The caliphs paid scholars to translate Greek, Persian and Sanskrit works into Arabic; many Greek books survive only because of it."},
 {d:3,t:"mem",st:"Al-Qarawiyyin",q:"Who founded al-Qarawiyyin in Fez in 859?",o:["Fatima al-Fihri, a merchant’s daughter","A Moroccan king","A Roman governor","A French scholar"],a:0,cc:"ma",why:"She used her inheritance to found a mosque that grew into a centre for law, grammar, astronomy and medicine."},
 {d:3,t:"mem",best:1,st:"Bologna, Paris and Oxford",q:"At the University of Bologna, traditionally dated to 1088, who was in charge?",o:["The students, who hired and even fined their teachers","The teachers","The Pope","The city council"],a:0,cc:"it",why:"Students banded together in a universitas, a guild. In Paris the teachers ran things instead, and Oxford followed Paris."},
 {d:3,t:"why",st:"Print, Protestants and reading",q:"Why did reading spread especially fast in Protestant lands?",o:["Reading the Bible yourself became a Protestant goal","Protestants invented printing","Catholic countries banned schools","Protestant books were free"],a:0,why:"Sweden’s church tested every household’s reading from the late 1600s, and by 1800 most Swedes could read."},
 {d:3,t:"mem",st:"School around the world",q:"Which country came top in maths, reading and science in the 2022 PISA tests?",o:["Singapore","Finland","The USA","The UK"],a:0,cc:"sg",why:"Japan, Korea, Taiwan and Estonia were close behind."},
 {d:3,t:"why",best:1,st:"School by radio",q:"Why did children in the Australian outback have lessons by two-way radio from 1951?",o:["They lived hundreds of kilometres from the nearest school","Teachers were not allowed outside","Radios were cheaper than books","Schools were closed for heat"],a:0,cc:"au",why:"Alice Springs School of the Air borrowed the Royal Flying Doctor Service’s radio network. Today lessons run over satellite internet."},
 {d:3,t:"map",st:"School by radio",q:"Click Alice Springs, whose School of the Air covers over a million square kilometres.",ll:[133.88,-23.7],km:400,cc:"au",why:"Alice Springs lies near the centre of Australia, in the Northern Territory."},
 {d:3,t:"why",st:"Libraries on camels",q:"Why does Kenya’s library service around Garissa bring books on camels?",o:["Families are herders who move, and camels cross sand and dry riverbeds where vans get stuck","Camels are cheaper than books","It is a tourist show","Roads are banned"],a:0,cc:"ke",why:"A library in one place would rarely be near the herders’ camps, so the library comes to them."},
 {d:4,t:"mem",best:1,st:"The manuscripts of Timbuktu",q:"When armed groups occupied Timbuktu in 2012, where did librarians smuggle some 350,000 manuscripts?",o:["Bamako","Dakar","Cairo","Paris"],a:0,cc:"ml",why:"Bamako, Mali’s capital, is about 700 km away. Some of the manuscripts are over 700 years old."},
 {d:4,t:"map",st:"The manuscripts of Timbuktu",q:"Click Timbuktu, where Saharan caravans brought salt, gold and books.",ll:[-3.0,16.77],km:400,cc:"ml",why:"Timbuktu lies near the Niger River on the southern edge of the Sahara, in Mali."},
 {d:4,t:"map",st:"Print, Protestants and reading",q:"Click the country that in 1961 sent about 250,000 volunteers into the countryside and taught over 700,000 adults to read in a year.",pick:["cu"],cc:"cu",why:"Cuba’s literacy campaign used many teenagers as teachers."},
 {d:4,t:"mem",st:"Cables under the sea",q:"The first transatlantic cable of 1858 ran from Valentia Island in which country?",o:["Ireland","Britain","France","Portugal"],a:0,cc:"ie",why:"It reached Heart’s Content in Newfoundland, about 3,000 km away. Queen Victoria’s 98-word greeting took about 16 hours, and the cable failed within a month."},
 {d:4,t:"why",best:1,st:"The Knowledge",q:"London cabbies must learn about 25,000 streets. Why does Manhattan need no such test?",o:["Manhattan is laid out on a grid; London grew as a tangle of merged villages","Manhattan has no taxis","New York uses only buses","Manhattan streets have no names"],a:0,cc:"gb",why:"London grew without a plan, so there is no simple pattern to learn; a grid is easy to navigate."},
 {d:4,t:"mem",st:"Libraries on camels",q:"A teacher, Luis Soriano, carried books to villages on two donkeys, Alfa and Beto. In which country?",o:["Colombia","Mexico","Spain","Peru"],a:0,cc:"co",why:"His travelling library became known as the ‘Biblioburro’."},
 {d:5,t:"mem",best:1,st:"The Knowledge",q:"What did a study that followed trainee London cabbies find?",o:["Those who passed grew more grey matter in the hippocampus; those who failed did not","All trainees’ brains shrank","Only older trainees changed","Nothing changed at all"],a:0,why:"The back of the hippocampus helps us find our way. Eleanor Maguire’s team showed learning the Knowledge changes the brain."},
 {d:5,t:"mem",st:"Exams for an empire",q:"Hanoi’s Temple of Literature, built in 1070, housed Vietnam’s first what?",o:["University","Printing press","Library of paper","Bank"],a:0,cc:"vn",why:"Korea and Vietnam adopted China’s exam system; Vietnam’s first university opened there in 1076."}
